WEBVTT

00:00:15.583 --> 00:00:17.542
- Aunt Lucy!
- Paddington!

00:00:17.667 --> 00:00:19.292
Aunt Lucy, we're here.

00:00:19.417 --> 00:00:20.958
- Hello, good afternoon!
- Oh...

00:00:21.542 --> 00:00:23.208
Aunt Lucy?

00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:24.792
Oh! Hello?

00:00:24.917 --> 00:00:26.958
(All) Hello.

00:00:27.083 --> 00:00:28.708
Please could you tell me the way
to Aunt Lucy's cabin?

00:00:29.208 --> 00:00:32.542
- Yes, it's that way, but I...
- Thank you.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.167
Aunt Lucy!

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.250
Aunt Lucy, it's me!

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:41.583
Aunt Lucy?

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:45.917
Aunt Lucy?

00:00:47.208 --> 00:00:48.625
Paddington...

00:00:49.083 --> 00:00:51.542
Missing? Whatever do you mean?

00:00:51.667 --> 00:00:53.750
She's gone, and we have no idea
where she is.

00:00:53.875 --> 00:00:55.125
What do you mean "gone"?

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:59.250
Well, she seems to have set off
on some sort of quest into the jungle.

00:00:59.375 --> 00:01:01.625
Now? But she knew we were coming.

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:03.583
Well, that's what's so mysterious.

00:01:03.708 --> 00:01:08.250
She was so excited to see you,
counting down the days to your arrival.

00:01:08.375 --> 00:01:11.708
But I'm... I'm afraid to say
that since I wrote,

00:01:11.833 --> 00:01:14.375
your aunt's behaviour
has become even more worrying.

00:01:16.542 --> 00:01:19.458
'She seemed to have been
researching something.'

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:25.250
Whatever it was,
she was obsessed with it.

00:01:25.375 --> 00:01:27.583
I mean, very secretive
about the whole thing.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.417
'And then we went to check on her...'

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:37.042
...and she wasn't there.

00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:39.500
This isn't like Aunt Lucy.

00:01:39.625 --> 00:01:42.875
Something's wrong.
We need to send out a search party!

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.833
Well, we already did, my dear.

00:01:48.458 --> 00:01:52.208
I'm afraid all they found were these,
washed down the river.

00:01:55.458 --> 00:01:57.292
Aunt Lucy's special bracelet.

00:01:57.417 --> 00:01:59.583
She'd never take this off, un...

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:01.500
...unless...

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.708
(Gasps)

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:07.208
Broken.

00:02:07.333 --> 00:02:08.500
Paddington...

00:02:08.625 --> 00:02:11.292
I don't know what's happened to her,
Mrs Brown...

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.958
...but she may be hurt, or in trouble.

00:02:15.083 --> 00:02:19.042
And she can't see without her glasses
and... I have to find her.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:25.792
Well, Paddington, I hate to say this,
but the Amazon is quite...

00:02:33.458 --> 00:02:35.458
...large.

00:02:38.125 --> 00:02:40.583
- Paddington...
- We have to try.

00:02:40.708 --> 00:02:43.000
Aunt Lucy would never give up on me.

00:02:43.125 --> 00:02:46.042
Alas, there's nothing any of us
can do tonight.

00:02:46.167 --> 00:02:48.750
And you must be very tired
after your journey.

00:02:48.875 --> 00:02:51.042
- The nuns will show you your rooms.
- Thank you.

00:02:51.167 --> 00:02:52.583
(Mrs Brown) Thank you so much.

00:02:58.208 --> 00:02:59.833
(Paddington) Hmm...

00:03:00.708 --> 00:03:05.500
All it takes to light the darkness
is one candle of faith.

00:03:08.875 --> 00:03:10.417
Something will turn up.

00:03:17.167 --> 00:03:21.000
Where are you, Aunt Lucy?
What were you looking for?

00:03:23.542 --> 00:03:27.958
If only you had left me
some sort of... clue.

00:03:32.875 --> 00:03:35.208
Oh, my goodness!
This must be it!

00:03:35.333 --> 00:03:37.708
- (Creaking)
- This floorboard's loose.

00:03:37.833 --> 00:03:40.833
Perhaps there's a secret compartment
or...

00:03:42.125 --> 00:03:44.083
Oh! Ooh!

00:03:45.167 --> 00:03:46.417
Mmm?

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:53.083
Huh?

00:03:53.792 --> 00:03:56.250
(Mr Brown) Yes, I'd like to report
a missing bear, please.

00:03:56.375 --> 00:04:00.625
Brown eyes, brown fur...
Well, in fact, just brown.

00:04:02.208 --> 00:04:06.542
Yes, we've lost a jungle bear
in... in the jungle.

00:04:06.667 --> 00:04:09.000
I-I'm not sure
I like your tone of voice.

00:04:09.458 --> 00:04:10.667
And a good day to you too, sir.

00:04:15.042 --> 00:04:16.208
- Morning.
- Morning.

00:04:16.333 --> 00:04:17.583
Any luck with the police?

00:04:17.708 --> 00:04:20.875
Apparently "too busy
looking for lost fish in the river".

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:27.625
It does seem odd, Aunt Lucy just leaving
like that, with no explanation.

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:30.167
Morning, everyone!
I found clue!

00:04:30.292 --> 00:04:31.875
I know where to look
for Aunt Lucy!

00:04:32.708 --> 00:04:35.417
It's a place called Rumi Rock,
and look...

00:04:36.167 --> 00:04:38.125
...it's only a few days upriver.

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:39.542
We can take breakfast with us.

00:04:39.667 --> 00:04:43.417
But wait, Paddington, what's going on?
Where did you get this old map?

00:04:43.542 --> 00:04:45.625
I found it in Aunt Lucy's room.

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:48.375
In fact, I think she rather
wanted me to find it.

00:04:49.042 --> 00:04:53.042
Rumi Rock? What makes you think
we should start the search there?

00:04:53.167 --> 00:04:55.833
Well, Aunt Lucy has made a note.

00:04:55.958 --> 00:04:57.167
"Start search here".

00:04:59.583 --> 00:05:00.417
Fair enough.

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:06.417
The Lord be praised!
I knew something would turn up.

00:05:06.542 --> 00:05:08.417
What is this Rumi Rock?

00:05:08.542 --> 00:05:11.875
It's a sacred Inca stone circle
deep in the jungle.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:13.708
And what's so special about it?

00:05:13.833 --> 00:05:14.750
No idea.

00:05:14.875 --> 00:05:17.083
Sacred Inca monuments
aren't really a nun thing.

00:05:18.583 --> 00:05:21.500
But it's a clue
to finding your Aunt Lucy.

00:05:21.625 --> 00:05:25.042
Seek out what she was looking for
and it may lead you to her.

00:05:25.625 --> 00:05:30.083
If anyone can do it,
it's you, young bear.

00:05:31.333 --> 00:05:33.292
Aunt Lucy always says,

00:05:33.417 --> 00:05:37.625
"When skies are grey
hope is the way."

00:05:38.542 --> 00:05:41.458
Right, well, just-just, you know...
hang on a minute.

00:05:41.583 --> 00:05:45.167
Surely someone from here
would be in a much better position to go...

00:05:45.292 --> 00:05:49.125
Oh, I don't permit myself or my nuns
to enter the jungle.

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:51.083
I find it somewhat...

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:55.333
...disquieting.

00:06:01.958 --> 00:06:02.958
(Chuckles nervously)

00:06:03.083 --> 00:06:05.708
I think it's best if I stay here
and tend my flock.

00:06:05.833 --> 00:06:09.792
But I commend your bravery
and I have faith that you will survive.

00:06:11.292 --> 00:06:12.458
That's reassuring.

00:06:12.583 --> 00:06:18.708
Mr Brown, if you feel you can't come...
I shall go on my own.

00:06:23.167 --> 00:06:27.125
Right. Well, obviously the Brown family
are clearly exactly the sort of people

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:30.167
with the requisite skill-set
to go looking for a lost bear in the jungle.

00:06:30.792 --> 00:06:32.667
- Thank you, Mr Brown.
- Hallelujah!

00:06:33.208 --> 00:06:34.250
Mmm? No... Hmm.

00:06:34.917 --> 00:06:37.500
Now, don't forget to take these.

00:06:37.625 --> 00:06:40.292
You can give them to your aunt
when you find her.

00:06:40.417 --> 00:06:42.583
- Thank you very much, Reverend Mother.
- (Mrs Bird) I'll man basecamp.

00:06:42.708 --> 00:06:46.458
I'll make sure everything's shipshape
for when she gets back.

00:06:47.042 --> 00:06:49.833
Mmm... Mary? One thing.

00:06:49.958 --> 00:06:54.042
Would you forgive an old nun
her comforts?

00:06:54.167 --> 00:06:57.542
St Christopher, patron saint of travel.

00:06:57.667 --> 00:07:00.417
I do believe
he will keep your family safe.

00:07:00.542 --> 00:07:01.583
Keep him close?

00:07:02.583 --> 00:07:04.458
Yes, of course.

00:07:04.583 --> 00:07:08.500
Gosh, thank you so much.

00:07:08.625 --> 00:07:10.667
Well, we need all the help
we can get.

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:14.708
Well, thank you.

00:07:16.208 --> 00:07:17.208
Thank you.

00:07:17.333 --> 00:07:18.833
I have to go now.

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:25.708
(Paddington) We just need to find a boat
that will take us upriver.

00:07:25.833 --> 00:07:28.167
- I can do my own suncream, Mum!
- Just behind your ears!

00:07:28.292 --> 00:07:30.458
Everyone, listen up.

00:07:30.583 --> 00:07:33.167
We are on the threshold
of the actual Amazon,

00:07:33.292 --> 00:07:34.458
- a place full of risk.
- (Honks horn) Careful!

00:07:34.583 --> 00:07:36.000
(Mrs Brown) Oh!

00:07:36.875 --> 00:07:38.458
So, we're gonna embrace that.

00:07:38.583 --> 00:07:40.875
Mr Brown, why are you walking like that?

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:43.417
- It's his hard walk.
- It's a perfectly normal walk.

00:07:43.542 --> 00:07:45.125
He does it
when the plumber comes round.

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:46.250
I do not!

00:07:46.708 --> 00:07:50.417
Right. Look, whatever boat
we manage to get,

00:07:50.542 --> 00:07:54.625
I don't think it's exactly
going to be the pride of the regatta, okay?

00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:56.708
- Er... Mr Brown?
- Huh?

00:07:56.833 --> 00:07:59.333
- Look.
- Mmm?

00:07:59.458 --> 00:08:02.625
(♪ Opera playing on gramophone)

00:08:05.167 --> 00:08:08.167
Oh, what a handsome...

00:08:08.292 --> 00:08:10.375
- Mmm?
- Boat.

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:14.292
Good idea, we'll ask the handsome captain.
Excuse me, sir.

00:08:14.417 --> 00:08:17.292
Is this boat for hire?

00:08:17.417 --> 00:08:18.958
For hire?

00:08:19.083 --> 00:08:23.917
Yeah, <i>señor</i> Bear...
and company.

00:08:24.042 --> 00:08:29.208
It is your lucky day!
This is the best boat on the river!

00:08:29.333 --> 00:08:34.958
Strong, smooth, easy on the eye
and... that's just the boat.

